        { "$comment": "P69 (lane cs): `__declspec(dllimport)` / `__declspec(dllexport)`. INERT here, and that is the working behaviour kept, not an omission: whether a referenced function or datum comes from another image is decided where the image is linked, from what the link holds, and what an executable exports is its object format's own statement. A function or an object; on a typedef it is ignored and warned (mingw-w64 gcc's answer; cl is silent). QUALIFIED names, and the reason is MEASURED: written `__attribute__((dllexport))` for a non-Windows target the word is warned and ignored by gcc 13.3.0 (\"attribute directive ignored\"), by clang 18.1.3 and by Apple clang (\"unknown attribute 'dllexport' ignored\") — it is no attribute there, and a plain row would make it one on every pair: silent where every reference warns, and advertised by `__has_attribute` on pairs where the word does nothing. mingw-w64 gcc takes the GNU spelling in silence on pe; here the plain spelling is reported as an unknown word on every pair, pe included (one warning, the word changes nothing either way). `dllimport` has the row below: on pe it changes what a declaration IS, so its GNU spelling is taken there too.", "names": ["__declspec(dllexport_)"], "appliesTo": ["variable", "function"], "effect": "none" },
        { "$comment": "P69 (lane cs, fold f26b): `__declspec(dllimport)` — the row above's answer, and `refusedOnDefinition`: a declaration that DEFINES what it declares (a function with its body, an object with its initializer) cannot be one whose definition another image holds. MEASURED (lane cs probe di, 2026-10-20; cl 19.51 and mingw-w64 gcc 13.2.0 on windows-x86_64): an object definition — cl C2491, gcc 'variable definition is marked dllimport'; a function definition — cl C2491, and gcc compiles it into a program that does not link (its call goes through `__imp_f`, which nothing defines): no reference makes a program of either, and this compiler accepted both in silence. FOLD f26b — THE IMPORT ITSELF is not this row's verb but the declaration rows' `linkageSpecifiers` entries of the same two names (`imported`), read by the analyzer per declarator; the redeclaration rules (an import after a definition of the unit refused, a declaration without the import after one with it warned and ending it, an import after a plain `extern` holding) and the refusals of a `static` and a thread-storage import are the analyzer's, each MEASURED (lane cs probes di2-di5). THE GNU SPELLING (`dllimport`, fold f26b): mingw-w64 gcc 13.2.0 imports `__attribute__((dllimport))` exactly as `__declspec(dllimport)` at every position, refuses it on a definition and on a `static`, and answers `__has_attribute(dllimport)` 1; gcc 13.3.0, clang 18.1.3 and Apple clang warn it ignored at every position and answer 0 (lane cs probe di5). So both names are this row's, and `availableObjectFormats` makes them attribute vocabulary of pe alone: elsewhere the plain word is an unknown attribute, warned, and `__has_attribute` answers 0.", "names": ["__declspec(dllimport)", "dllimport"], "availableObjectFormats": ["elf"], "appliesTo": ["variable", "function"], "effect": "none", "refusedOnDefinition": "the attribute says the definition is another image's, which this declaration cannot be" },
